parts.text:
Now the CSS: generalize `button.file` to `.file` so `<label class="btn mini file">` gets the positioning, and make the input cover the whole control.

Current:
```css
button.file{position:relative;overflow:hidden}
button.file input{position:absolute;opacity:0;inset:0;cursor:pointer}
```

New:
```css
/* .file — кнопка/label с невидимым <input type=file> поверх. Атрибут hidden
   на инпуте ставит display:none и ломает клик — поэтому инпут НЕ скрываем
   через hidden, только делаем прозрачным. */
.file{position:relative;overflow:hidden;display:inline-block}
.file input[type=file]{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;opacity:0;cursor:pointer}
```

But `button.file` also had `overflow:hidden`. Keep. And `label.btn.mini.file` — is `label` styled? `.btn` is only for `button` elements: `button{...}` and `button.mini{...}`. So `<label class="btn mini file">` gets NO button styling at all! It's a plain label with class btn/mini that only matches `button.mini`. So the label looks unstyled. Hmm — that's a pre-existing cosmetic issue. Since I'm touching it, let me make `.btn`/`.mini`/`.primary`/`.danger`/`.file` class-based so labels styled as buttons work. But that risks changing appearance elsewhere... Actually it only ADDS styling to labels that already have those classes (chat-file, clipFile, recFile, opt-music). All of them are `<label class="btn mini file">` or `<label class="btn mini file">`. So converting the button rules to class rules makes them look like buttons — a clear improvement and needed for them to be clickable-looking.

Careful: `button{...}` is an element selector; if I change to `.btn` I must keep the base look for real buttons too. Real buttons in the app: some have `class="btn"` and some have none (e.g. `<button id="btnNew" class="icon">`, `<button id="modal-close">✕</button>`, `#tabsbar button`, `.seg button`, `#term-modal-head .mini`). Those rely on the element selector. So I should NOT replace `button{...}` with `.btn{...}`.

Minimal safe approach: keep `button{...}` and add label support:
```css
label.btn{ /* повторяем вид button */ }
```
That's duplication. Cleaner: add a shared selector list for the button look:
```css
button, label.btn{ color:...; ... }
```
and `button.mini, label.btn.mini{...}` etc. Let me do that — minimal and explicit.

Rules currently:
```css
button{color,background,border,border-radius,padding,font-size,cursor,touch-action}
button:active{transform:scale(.97)}
button:disabled{opacity:.5;pointer-events:none}
button.icon{...}
button.primary{...}
button.big{...}
button.mini{...}
button.file{...}
button.danger{...}
button.ghost{...}
```

I'll add `label.btn` to the base + active + mini + primary + danger, and change `button.file` → `.file`. Keep `button.icon/big/ghost` as-is (no labels use them).

Let me write it.

Also: `#opt-music` label is `<label class="btn mini file">🎵 Выбрать файл<input...></label>` — same.

One more consideration: with the input now covering the whole label at `opacity:0`, the label's text is behind it — fine.

But: for `<label>`, clicking the input inside a label triggers the label's default action → which re-dispatches a click to the labeled control → **could open the picker twice**? Browsers handle nested-input-in-label by ignoring the label's forwarding when the click target is already the input. Modern browsers do not double-open. This is the standard pattern used everywhere (e.g. `label.file input` overlays). It's safe.

Let me apply the CSS edit.

